{
  "task": "format_questions_to_latex",
  "questions": [
    {
      "source_id": "1",
      "stem": "A camper sets up a tent at the origin of a coordinate map. Later, they spot a trail marker at the position vector $\\vec r = 7\\hat{i}+11\\hat{j}$, where distances are measured in kilometers. Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The distance between the trail marker and the tent is 18 km.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This corresponds to adding components, $|7|+|11|$, which is the Manhattan distance, not the straight-line distance. The physical distance on a flat map is found using $\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The distance between the trail marker and the tent is 11 km.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses the distance with the $y$-component of the position vector. The distance must include both the $x$ and $y$ components via the Pythagorean theorem."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The distance between the trail marker and the tent is 7 km.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses the distance with the $x$-component of the position vector. The magnitude depends on both components, not just one."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "The distance from the origin is the magnitude of the position vector: $|\\vec r|=\\sqrt{7^2+11^2}=\\sqrt{170}\\approx 13.04\\ \\text{km}$. Since $13.04$ km is not equal to $18$, $11$, or $7$ km, none of A–C is correct."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "2",
      "stem": "If the displacement of an object is given by the function $\\vec{s}(t)=\\left((0.90\\,\\mathrm{m/s^3})t^3-(4.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2})t^2\\right)\\hat{i}$, which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "This is a constant-acceleration motion.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Taking two time derivatives shows $a(t)$ contains a term proportional to $t$, so it is not constant. A $t^3$ term in $s(t)$ generally produces a time-dependent acceleration."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at $t=0\\,\\mathrm{s}$ is $8.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Differentiate twice: $\\vec v(t)=\\dfrac{d\\vec s}{dt}=(2.70t^2-8.0t)\\hat i$ and $\\vec a(t)=\\dfrac{d\\vec v}{dt}=(5.40t-8.0)\\hat i$. At $t=0$, $\\vec a(0)=-8.0\\hat i$, so the magnitude is $|\\vec a(0)|=8.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration is $4.0\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This incorrectly reads the coefficient of the $t^2$ term in position as the acceleration. Acceleration comes from the second derivative of position, not directly from a position coefficient."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "The magnitude of the acceleration at $t=0\\,\\mathrm{s}$ is $0\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This mistake typically comes from assuming the acceleration must vanish at $t=0$ or from differentiating incorrectly. The second derivative gives a nonzero constant term at $t=0$."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "source_id": "3",
      "stem": "An object takes $5.0\\,\\mathrm{s}$ to move from position A ($\\vec{r}_A=-6.0\\hat{i}-1.0\\hat{j}$) to position B ($\\vec{r}_B=4.0\\hat{i}+7.0\\hat{j}$), where length units are in meters. Which of the following statements is correct?",
      "options": [
        {
          "key": "A",
          "text": "The speed at $t=0$ must be $2.6\\,\\mathrm{m/s}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "This confuses an average rate (like $|\\Delta\\vec r|/\\Delta t$) with the instantaneous speed at $t=0$. Many different motions can connect the same endpoints in $5.0$ s with different initial speeds."
        },
        {
          "key": "B",
          "text": "The acceleration at $t=0$ must be $0.51\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "With only two position vectors and a total time, the acceleration at a specific time is not uniquely determined. A numerical value like $0.51\\,\\mathrm{m/s^2}$ would require extra assumptions (e.g., constant acceleration and more data)."
        },
        {
          "key": "C",
          "text": "The moving distance is $12.8\\,\\mathrm{m}$.",
          "is_correct": true,
          "explanation": "Compute the displacement: $\\Delta\\vec r=\\vec r_B-\\vec r_A=(4-(-6))\\hat i+(7-(-1))\\hat j=10\\hat i+8\\hat j$. Its magnitude is $|\\Delta\\vec r|=\\sqrt{10^2+8^2}=\\sqrt{164}\\approx 12.8\\,\\mathrm{m}$, so this statement matches the straight-line separation between A and B."
        },
        {
          "key": "D",
          "text": "None of the above is correct.",
          "is_correct": false,
          "explanation": "Option C is correct because the magnitude of the displacement between the given positions is about $12.8\\,\\mathrm{m}$. Therefore it is not true that none of the statements are correct."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "requirements": {
    "include_document_preamble": true,
    "include_macros": [
      "\\QBlock",
      "\\Correct",
      "\\Wrong"
    ],
    "format_all_questions_with_macros": true,
    "proper_math_formatting": true,
    "filename": "isomorphic_questions.tex"
  }
}

